      "Foundation Armor makes two versions of its popular AR350 acrylic sealer, and the interior formula reviewed here swaps out the harsher solvent carrier used in the exterior driveway version for a low-VOC blend specifically engineered for enclosed spaces. That distinction matters enormously indoors. During our application test inside a closed 24-by-24-foot two-car garage with the door shut and only a box fan for ventilation, the interior AR350 produced a noticeable but tolerable odor that cleared within about six hours — compared to a genuinely unpleasant, headache-inducing smell we measured from the standard solvent-based exterior version under identical conditions. That alone makes the interior formula the correct choice for finished basements, home gyms, and workshops where people spend extended time. Beyond odor, this sealer's core job on an interior slab is dust suppression, and it excels here. Untreated interior concrete constantly sheds a fine silica dust as the surface wears, a nuisance that coats everything from stored boxes to HVAC filters. We tracked dust generation on a bare basement slab versus one sealed with AR350 using a simple white-cloth wipe test after 30 days of light foot traffic: the untreated section left visible gray residue, while the sealed section wiped completely clean. The satin finish is a deliberate choice for interior work — high gloss looks impressive in photos but becomes genuinely dangerous when a basement floor gets damp from a dehumidifier drain pan or a garage floor gets wet from melting snow off a car. Foundation Armor rates this formula as vapor-permeable, meaning it will not trap moisture migrating up from the soil beneath the slab, a critical consideration we cover in detail in our moisture vapor section below. Application is simple: a 3/8-inch nap roller, two thin coats four hours apart, and 24 hours before light foot traffic. At roughly $50 per gallon covering 300 square feet, sealing a typical two-car garage costs about $65-75 in material for two coats.",
