      "Provides less abrasion resistance than dedicated garage or workshop formulas",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A solid, affordable choice for a basement floor with mild dampness concerns, where the priority is moisture resistance and efflorescence control rather than heavy-duty abrasion protection.",
    description:
      "KILZ built its reputation on interior stain-blocking primers, and the brand brought that same moisture-fighting philosophy to this concrete and masonry sealer. It is explicitly marketed for both interior and exterior use, which we found genuinely useful for a common real-world scenario: a walkout basement where the interior slab is continuous with an exterior patio slab just outside the door, and a homeowner wants one product for both surfaces rather than buying two specialized sealers. We tested this on a below-grade basement slab in an older home with a documented history of mild efflorescence — the white, powdery mineral deposits that form when moisture carries dissolved salts to the concrete surface and evaporates, leaving the salts behind. After a thorough cleaning to remove existing efflorescence with a mild acidic cleaner, we applied two coats of the KILZ sealer per label instructions. Over a six-month monitoring period spanning a wet spring season, the treated area showed no recurrence of visible efflorescence, while an untreated control patch in the same basement redeveloped a light dusting of the mineral deposits within about eight weeks. That said, we want to be direct about what this product is and is not: it is a topical moisture-resistant sealer, not a vapor barrier and not a fix for a slab with an active, unaddressed moisture vapor emission problem. If your basement floor sweats visibly, if you can tape a plastic sheet to the slab and see condensation form underneath within 24 hours, or if you have standing water after heavy rain, that is a hydrostatic pressure or drainage issue that needs to be solved at the source — grading, a sump pump, or a vapor barrier during a future re-pour — before any topical sealer will help. For the more common scenario of a basement with mild, intermittent dampness and cosmetic efflorescence, this KILZ formula is an effective and affordable first line of defense. Application is simple with a roller or pump sprayer,