        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          How to Seal an Interior Concrete Floor: Step-by-Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sealing an interior slab correctly is less about the product and more about preparation and patience. Because you are working in an enclosed space, ventilation, dry time, and moisture testing all matter more than they would on an outdoor project. Here is the process we followed on every test floor in this guide.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 1: Test for Moisture Vapor Emission</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Before doing anything else, run the 24-hour plastic sheet test described above on at least two or three spots around the floor, prioritizing areas near exterior walls and low points where water might collect. If you find condensation, plan on a vapor-permeable sealer or a lithium densifier rather than a thick impermeable topical film, and consider addressing exterior drainage or grading first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 2: Degrease and Deep Clean</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Garage and workshop floors almost always have some oil or grease contamination, even if it is not visibly obvious, and any residue will prevent proper sealer adhesion. Scrub the entire floor with a citrus-based degreaser and a stiff bristle brush, then rinse thoroughly with clean water. For basement floors, a simple TSP substitute cleaner and a wet-dry vacuum for rinse water works well since a pressure washer is often impractical indoors. Allow the slab to dry completely — a minimum of 48 hours with good airflow, longer in humid conditions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 3: Ventilate the Space</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Even low-VOC and water-based sealers benefit from active airflow during application and curing. Open all doors and windows if possible, and run a box fan positioned to exhaust air out of the space rather than recirculate it. For a basement without exterior access, run a fan in the stairwell and keep the area unoccupied during application and for several hours afterward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 4: Apply Densifier First, If Using One</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            If you are using a lithium silicate densifier, apply it now as a flood coat with a low-pressure pump sprayer, keep the surface visibly wet for the manufacturer's specified dwell time (typically 15-30 minutes), then squeegee off any excess pooled liquid and let the floor dry fully, usually 24 hours, before moving to a topical sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 5: Apply Thin, Even Topical Coats</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Using a 3/8-inch nap roller, apply the topical sealer in thin, even passes, working in sections of about 75-100 square feet and maintaining a wet edge to avoid lap marks. Two thin coats, applied four to six hours apart per the label, produce a more even, durable finish than one thick coat. Keep the floor free of foot traffic for at least 24 hours and avoid parking a vehicle or rolling heavy equipment on it for a full 72 hours to allow the film to cure and harden completely.
          </p>
        </div>

