          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is moisture vapor emission and why does it matter before sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Moisture vapor emission is water vapor migrating up through a concrete slab from the soil beneath it, especially common in basements and slab-on-grade construction. If you seal a slab with an impermeable film sealer while significant moisture vapor is present, the trapped moisture can cause blistering, delamination, or a milky white haze under the sealer. Before sealing any basement or ground-level interior floor, perform a simple plastic sheet test: tape an 18-by-18-inch sheet of plastic to the slab and check after 24 hours. If condensation forms underneath, address the moisture source or choose a vapor-permeable, breathable sealer rather than a fully impermeable film.
